      "text": "To determine the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we first need to find the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\nWe start by performing the division \\(5 \\div 14\\):\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.357142857142\\ldots\n\\]\nThe decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.\\overline{357142}\\), which means the digits \\(357142\\) repeat indefinitely.\n\nTo find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit, we need to determine the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit within the repeating block \\(357142\\). We do this by finding the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6 (since the repeating block has 6 digits).\n\nWe calculate:\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\nA remainder of 0 indicates that the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block \\(357142\\). The last digit of \\(357142\\) is \\(2\\).\n\nTherefore, the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
